        "title": "Internal changes",
        "position": "lower table row 2 left label",
        "count": 5,
        "cells": [
          "High in starch, low in sugar, with firm flesh and low in moisture content.",
          "As starch gradually converts into sugar, the sugar content rises, the pulp begins to soften, and the moisture content becomes moderate.",
          "High sugar content, with most of the starch converted, resulting in soft, sweet flesh and a slightly high water content.",
          "The sugar content is very high, the flesh is too soft, and it loses a lot of moisture, resulting in a sticky texture.",
          "Bacterial/mold growth destroys nutrients, decomposes tissues, and produces harmful substances."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Consumption suggestions",
        "position": "lower table row 3 left label",
        "count": 5,
        "cells": [
          "It is not recommended for consumption; it has a raw and astringent taste and is not suitable for eating.",
          "At its peak, it is sweet, soft, and chewy, offering the best taste and rich nutrition.",
          "It's delicious, with a rich aroma and a soft, chewy texture, perfect for those who prefer a softer taste.",
          "It is recommended to consume it as soon as possible. Its texture is quite soft; it can be used in desserts and baking, but it is not recommended to eat large quantities directly.",
          "Do not consume. It has an unusual odor, is moldy, and may cause discomfort."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Time reference (room temperature storage)",
        "position": "bottom timeline",
        "count": 5,
        "labels": [
          "0–3 days immature period",
          "4–7 days to mature",
          "8–10 days past maturity",
          "11–13 days of over-ripe decline",
          "More than 14 days of spoilage period"
